WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.512
Here's the root decision.

2
00:00:01.512 --> 00:00:04.241
A brand-new cloud root means every server,

3
00:00:04.241 --> 00:00:07.612
gateway and device has to
learn to trust a new chain.

4
00:00:07.612 --> 00:00:12.392
Anchoring the cloud issuer under the root
you already have keeps that trust intact,

5
00:00:12.392 --> 00:00:16.687
uses one of the tenant's three cloud
authorities, and leaves an easy way back.

6
00:00:16.687 --> 00:00:20.476
That's the program's recommendation
for an established hierarchy.

7
00:00:20.476 --> 00:00:22.650
But it carries one lasting cost.

8
00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:26.569
The offline root's revocation
list now has to stay published at

9
00:00:26.569 --> 00:00:30.187
a plain web address that Entra
ID can reach, or sign-ins fail.

10
00:00:30.187 --> 00:00:35.512
So the decision for the PKI administrators,
inside the security office's window:

11
00:00:35.512 --> 00:00:39.788
run that list as a production
service, with every ceremony dated.

12
00:00:39.788 --> 00:00:41.660
Servers still need an issuer.
